Here is a C# .net example of contract test using the PexMethod. The test generates several method call sequences (based on the assume under test) for all the methods in the class. It then executes each method call and verifies that it obeys the precondition and postcondition of the method.

For instance, if we have a "Divide" method with precondition for the denominator to not be zero and postcondition of the result being an integer, Pex will generate several different calls (for different denominators like 1, 2, 0, -1, etc.) and make sure the method behaves in correct way (doesn’t break the precondition and postcondition). 
There are many other powerful features in Pex like generation of various exception scenarios, black-box testing, shrinking paths, etc.

To summarize, using Pex it’s possible to unit-test your code for a variety of scenarios (like edge cases, random inputs etc.), it identifies flaky or broken tests, and helps you create better designs by learning from the existing tests.


Here is a simple example of how we could test PUT_AddContract method.

Consider we have an ADD_PUT method as shown below -

    public int ADD_PUT(int a, int b) 
    {
        return a + b;
    }

We can test it with Pex method as -

    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_ADD_TEST(int a, int b) 
    {
        int result = ADD_PUT(a, b);
    
        // We add assumptions to handle edge cases
        AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(a == int.MinValue && b < 0); // Handling overflow case
    
        // In Pex, Precondition is same as an assumption in method
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(a <= int.MaxValue - b); // int overflow
    
        // Testing postcondition
        Assert.AreEqual(a + b, result);
    }

The Pex code will automatically generate test scenarios based on the assumptions we make and the assertions we make. It tries handling all the edge cases for us.

So, by annotating our method with PexMethod attribute Pex will automatically generate tests for different inputs. If your method is expected to throw exception, then it will also handle that. It will also shrink the failing test if possible. Pex greatly improves the product quality by making sure unit tests are thorough and handle as many edge cases as possible.

Pex also comes out with a feature where it provides the method call paths that cause the failure which are minimal which help in debugging the issue. 

Remember this feature doesn't fix all testing issues, its a very good tool to assist in manual testing by reducing the test effort and time. The final testing should be done by human testers who can also understand the business need and business logic of the application. 

I hope this helps, do let me know if you need more information. Happy testing 😃.

Please note that Pex is no longer supported, the above information is provided based on the information available as of the time of this article being published."
}
